# Cloud Functions: deploy rules (ikm-marketplace)

## The constraint: a 20 vCPU Cloud Run quota

Every gen-2 function is a Cloud Run service. This project has **88** of them, all with
`maxScale=3` and `startup-cpu-boost=true`, and `us-central1` has a Cloud Run CPU quota
(`run.googleapis.com/cpu_allocation`) of only **20 vCPU** shared by all of them.

A full-functions deploy starts containers for all ~100 functions at once, overruns the quota, and
fails almost every function. The damage is not just that deploy: the started containers sit idle
for **~20–30 minutes**, holding the whole 20 vCPU quota, so deploys keep failing during that
window — including single-function ones. Measured: 21 idle instances vs. a 20 vCPU limit.

Once those instances drain, small batches deploy fine. So the failure is temporary, but it is
self-inflicted and wastes half an hour.

## NEVER run a full-`functions` deploy

```bash
# ❌ BAD — saturates the quota, fails ~every function, blocks the next ~30 min of work
firebase deploy --only functions
```

Name the functions you changed, and keep a batch to **2–3**:

```bash
# ✅ GOOD — small named batch
node ~/.npm/_npx/7750544ccf494d8b/node_modules/firebase-tools/lib/bin/firebase.js deploy \
  --only functions:markOrderAsSent,functions:markOrderAsReceived \
  --project ikm-marketplace
```

If a batch hits the quota error, **stop**. Do not retry and do not try a smaller batch — the
quota needs time to drain. Check the instance count (below) and come back when it is low.

## Decoding the failure

A wall of `Failed to update function <name> in region us-central1` looks like broken code. It is
not. The real reason is only in `firebase-debug.log`:

```
Could not create or update Cloud Run service <name>, Container Healthcheck failed.
Quota exceeded for total allowable CPU per project per region.
```

Never report this to the user as a code, build, or type error. Say it is the Cloud Run CPU quota.

### Other errors seen on schedule functions

`Error: Error generating the service identity for eventarc.googleapis.com.` — transient. The
Eventarc service agent already exists in IAM and the API is enabled, so this is not a config
problem and no fix is needed: retry the deploy. Verified twice.

## Runtime: Node.js 20 is almost dead

The CLI warns on every deploy:

```
Runtime Node.js 20 was deprecated on 2026-04-30 and will be decommissioned on 2026-10-30,
after which you will not be able to deploy without upgrading.
```

After **2026-10-30** no function can be deployed until `"engines": { "node": "20" }` in
`functions/package.json` is upgraded (to nodejs22 or later) and redeployed. Flag this to the user
rather than drifting past the date. Deploying is not currently blocked by it.

## Check headroom before and after deploying

`gcloud` is not on `PATH` — use `export PATH="$HOME/google-cloud-sdk/bin:$PATH"`.

```bash
# running instances in the region — keep this well under 20 before deploying
TOKEN=$(gcloud auth print-access-token)
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://monitoring.googleapis.com/v3/projects/ikm-marketplace/timeSeries?filter=metric.type%3D%22run.googleapis.com%2Fcontainer%2Finstance_count%22&interval.startTime=$(date -u -d '10 minutes ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)&interval.endTime=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
```

Quota vs. usage for the region: Cloud console → IAM & Admin → Quotas → filter
`run.googleapis.com/cpu_allocation` in `us-central1`.

## Deleting failed revisions does NOT free the quota

Tried and verified: 49 of 124 quota-failed revisions were deleted and the quota was unaffected —
failed revisions do not run containers, so they hold no CPU. It is harmless housekeeping, not a
fix, so do not reach for it to unblock a deploy. The other 75 cannot be deleted at all; Cloud Run
refuses with `FAILED_PRECONDITION` when the revision is the service's latest or is actively
serving. Note `gcloud run revisions delete` accepts only **one** revision per call.

## The durable fix (needs the user)

Batching only works while there is headroom. The real fix is a quota increase for
`run.googleapis.com/cpu_allocation` in `us-central1` (20 → ~100 vCPU), requested from the Cloud
console. Suggest this rather than endless small deploys.

## Housekeeping

- **A deploy ships everything on disk**, not just your edit: check `git status -- functions/`
  and commit first so there is a rollback point.
- Confirm the project is `ikm-marketplace` (see `.firebaserc`).
- `predeploy` runs `lint` then `build`; both must pass first.
- Delete `firebase-debug.log` afterwards — it is generated, untracked, and holds live
  environment values including secrets in plaintext.
